---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import ComponentPreview from '../../components/ComponentPreview.astro';

const configCode = `// decks/my-talk/deck.config.js
export default {
  title: 'My talk',
  theme: '../../tokens.css',       // href, relative to the OUTPUT file
  bundle: '../../dist/index.js',   // rikiki bundle, relative to the OUTPUT file
  transition: 'slide',             // optional · sets transition on deck-root
  slides: [
    'parts/cover.html',
    'parts/intro.md',
    'parts/closing.html',
  ],
};`;

const partialHtml = `<!-- decks/my-talk/parts/cover.html -->
<deck-cover brand="my-talk" speaker="You">
  <h1>Hello</h1>
  <p class="sub">One slide per idea, one file per slide.</p>
</deck-cover>`;

const partialMd = `<!-- decks/my-talk/parts/intro.md · one file, many slides -->
## First slide

Plain markdown. One idea here.

---

## Second slide

A line with just \`---\` starts a new slide.
Use \`***\` if you want a rule inside a slide.`;

const partialMdPreview = `<deck-feature>
  <deck-md>## First slide\n\nPlain markdown. One idea here.</deck-md>
</deck-feature>
<deck-feature>
  <deck-md>## Second slide\n\nA separator in the source starts this slide.</deck-md>
</deck-feature>`;

const runCmd = `npx rikiki assemble deck.config.js

# explicit output, or - for stdout
npx rikiki assemble deck.config.js out/my-talk.html

# writes my-talk.html next to the config · a normal rikiki deck`;
---

<Docs
  title="Multi-file decks"
  eyebrow="Guides"
  description="Split a long talk across small files and stitch them into one deck at build time. Reuse fragments between decks. Zero runtime weight added."
>

<h2 id="why">Why</h2>
<p>
  A single <code>index.html</code> is fine until a talk gets long. Past a
  point you want one slide per file, you want to reorder without scrolling
  through a wall of markup, and you want to reuse the same intro across two
  decks. That's what the assembler is for. It runs at build time and emits a
  plain rikiki deck. Nothing extra ships to the browser.
</p>

<h2 id="config">The manifest</h2>
<p>
  A deck is a <code>deck.config.js</code> (or <code>.json</code>): the theme,
  the bundle, an optional transition, and the ordered list of parts.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="decks/my-talk/deck.config.js" lang="js" code={configCode} />
<p>
  <code>theme</code> and <code>bundle</code> are hrefs written into the output
  file, so they're relative to where the assembled deck lands, not to the
  config.
</p>

<h2 id="partials">The parts</h2>
<p>
  Two kinds of fragment, picked by extension:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong><code>.html</code></strong> · one or more <code>deck-*</code> elements, inlined as-is.</li>
  <li><strong><code>.md</code></strong> · one file, one or many slides. A line that is just <code>---</code> splits the file into separate slides. Each chunk becomes a <code>deck-feature</code> with a <code>deck-md</code> inside. Use <code>***</code> for a rule inside a slide.</li>
</ul>
<CodeBlock label="parts/cover.html" lang="html" code={partialHtml} />
<ComponentPreview code={partialHtml} full />
<CodeBlock label="parts/intro.md" code={partialMd} />
<ComponentPreview code={partialMdPreview} full />

<h2 id="build">Build it</h2>
<p>
  Point the assembler at the config. It concatenates the parts inside one
  <code>deck-root</code> and writes the deck next to the config.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="assemble" code={runCmd} />
<p>
  The output is a normal rikiki deck. Open it in a browser, serve it from any
  static host, present it. Reorder the <code>slides</code> array, run the
  command again, and you've got the new order.
</p>

<h2 id="bundle">Bundling an assembled deck</h2>
<p>
  Want a single self-contained file? <code>rikiki bundle</code> takes an
  assembled deck the same as any other, on one condition: the deck has to
  reference rikiki with the <code>rikiki/...</code> path style that the
  bundler rewrites. That is the default in the config, and also what
  <code>rikiki init</code> writes. Point <code>theme</code> or
  <code>bundle</code> at bare <code>../../</code> relatives instead and the
  deck serves fine on its own, but the assemble command warns that those
  references will stay external.
</p>

</Docs>
